course: dictionary
lesson: 10662
title: Unlace
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To unlace means to undo the laces of something, such as shoes, boots or a corset. The word is built from un- (undo) and lace (a cord pulled through holes). After a muddy walk you unlace your boots so you can take them off.
explain: Unlace can also mean to loosen or open something by pulling out its laces. In old stories, a lady's dress was laced up tightly, and a fainting character might have her stays unlaced so she could breathe.
explain: Read the context: «Hedgehog unlaces Tortoise's back-plates so he can bend and curl a tiny bit.» Hedgehog is loosening the plates so Tortoise can move. Try your meaning in the sentence: undo the laces so he can bend, and it still makes sense.
fact: Unlace is a verb: unlace, unlaces, unlaced, unlacing.
fact: «Unlace» means to undo the laces of something, such as shoes, boots or a corset.
fact: «Unlace your shoes» and «unlace your boots» are useful phrases to remember.
fact: The past form is «unlaced», as in «She unlaced her boots.»
fact: Lace up is the opposite of unlace: «Lace up your boots before you go out.»
exercise: We unlace our boots after a muddy walk. What does unlace mean? | To wash the boots | To paint the boots | *To undo the laces | To tie a bow very tightly
exercise: «Hedgehog unlaces Tortoise's back-plates so he can bend and curl a tiny bit.» Why does Hedgehog unlace them? | To tie them tighter | So Tortoise can run faster than a hare | To make the plates shine | *So Tortoise can bend and curl a little
exercise: «The old lady's dress was laced up tight, and her maid unlaced it.» Find and copy the word that means the opposite of laced up. | *unlaced | maid | dress | tight
test: Yesterday Sam ___ his boots before bed. Which word fits the gap? | unlaces | unlace | *unlaced | unlacing
test: Which phrase is the opposite of «unlace your boots»? | Unlock the door | Untie your hair | Tie your hands | *Lace up your boots
exam: Which means the same as unlace? | To grow long and thin | To paint a picture on a wall | *To loosen or open something by pulling out its laces | To fall fast asleep
